## Discrete Picard Condition (DPC)

The DPC requires that, on average, the signal-coefficient absolute values |u_i^T y_exact| decay at least as fast as sigma_i.  In the noisy data the noise projection eta_i = u_i^T noise is approximately constant at sigma_noise ~ 0.0268, while sigma_i shrinks geometrically over fifteen decades.  Consequently the regularised ratio |beta_i|/sigma_i grows without bound in the noise-dominated tail (e.g. ~10^10 by i=200), so the DPC **does not hold** globally for this noisy data.  It holds only approximately for the first ~32 components (signal region).

